    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Many property insurance disputes are not about coverage at all. The insurer agrees that hail damaged the roof or that a frozen pipe burst. The disagreement is over price, quantity, or scope: repair versus replace, how many squares, which line items, what local labor really costs. For that kind of dispute, most Texas property policies include an appraisal clause. <strong>Insurance appraisal in San Antonio</strong> works the same way it does across Texas: a contractual process to determine the amount of loss. This page explains how it typically works, what it costs, and when it may or may not make sense. It is general information, not legal advice; your policy language controls.</p>

<h2>Disputes Suited to Insurance Appraisal</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof disputes</strong> over repair vs. replacement, the number of slopes, or which accessories are included. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Hail and wind claims</strong> where both sides agree on the event but not the scope. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">San Antonio hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Water, freeze, and fire claims</strong> with disagreements over drying, cleaning, or rebuild costs.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial claims</strong> with large gaps in unit pricing or quantities across many buildings. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Underpaid claims</strong> where negotiation has stalled. See <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How the Appraisal Process Usually Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Disagreement on amount.</strong> The parties have exchanged estimates and cannot agree on the amount of loss.</li>
<li><strong>Written demand.</strong> Either party demands appraisal in writing, following the procedure in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Each side names an appraiser.</strong> The policy usually requires a competent and impartial (or disinterested) appraiser, named within a stated number of days.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers select an umpire.</strong> If they cannot agree, many policies allow either party to ask a court to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers evaluate the loss.</strong> They inspect the property, exchange estimates, and try to reach agreement.</li>
<li><strong>Award.</strong> If the appraisers agree, they sign an award. If not, they submit their differences to the umpire. An award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</li>
</ol>
<p>Joseph Dittman, a licensed Texas public adjuster, is also a certified insurance appraiser and insurance umpire, and his background includes carrier-side adjusting. That gives us a working understanding of how appraisal looks from several sides of the table.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Public-Adjuster-vs.-Insurance-Adjuster-for-Hail-Claims-in-Texas-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster showing an estimate on a laptop to a homeowner couple" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Before invoking appraisal, it helps to walk through the disputed items and the evidence behind each one.</figcaption></figure>

