<h2>When Appraisal May Make Sense in San Antonio</h2>
<p>Appraisal tends to fit best when the remaining dispute is narrow and measurable. After large Bexar County hail events, like the April 2016 storm across northwest San Antonio, common disputes include whether a roof should be repaired or replaced, whether discontinued shingles can be matched, and whether unit prices reflect what local roofers were actually charging when demand was high. Water and freeze claims, such as those after Winter Storm Uri, often turn on how much drying, demolition, and rebuild work was needed.</p>
<p>Appraisal tends to fit poorly when the insurer has denied the claim outright, when an exclusion is squarely at issue, or when the gap between estimates is small compared with the cost of appraisers and an umpire. Before anyone sends a demand, list the disputed items, attach the evidence for each, and confirm the disagreement is truly about amount.</p>

<h2>Pros, Cons, and Costs</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Potential advantages</th><th>Potential drawbacks</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Often faster and less formal than litigation</td><td>Generally does not decide coverage disputes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focused on the actual cost of repair</td><td>Each side pays its own appraiser and usually shares the umpire's fee</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decided by people with construction and claims knowledge</td><td>The award may come in lower than you hoped</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can resolve a stalled negotiation</td><td>An award can limit later options on the amount</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>After an award, the insurer typically applies your deductible, prior payments, and any depreciation terms in the policy. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how those adjustments work.</p>

<h2>Common Appraisal Issues</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coverage vs. amount.</strong> If the insurer denies that a loss is covered, appraisal usually cannot resolve that. See <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Appraiser impartiality.</strong> Policies often require impartial appraisers, and challenges can arise.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection.</strong> Delays in agreeing on an umpire can slow the process.</li>
<li><strong>Unclear awards.</strong> Awards that do not itemize or separate replacement cost, depreciation, and categories can create later disputes about payment.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Appraisal vs. Litigation</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a contract process about price and scope. Litigation is a court process that can address coverage, breach of contract, and statutory claims, and it requires a Texas attorney. Weather-related claim lawsuits in Texas also require written pre-suit notice at least 61 days before filing under Chapter 542A. Public adjusters are not attorneys; if you are weighing litigation or have questions about the legal effect of an award, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>How a San Antonio Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>We can prepare the detailed Xactimate estimate and documentation appraisal depends on, review your policy's appraisal clause, explain the tradeoffs before you invoke it, and help you identify a qualified appraiser. An <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> is often the right first step. We cannot guarantee any appraisal result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Follow the policy's written demand procedure and deadlines.</li>
<li>Select an appraiser who meets the policy's requirements.</li>
<li>Pay your appraiser and your share of the umpire's costs as the policy provides.</li>
<li>Make the property available for inspection by both appraisers and the umpire.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not every policy includes an appraisal clause, and wording varies. Awards remain subject to policy limits, deductibles, and exclusions. If you are unsure whether appraisal fits your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
